& [CoreConclusion] is supported after replacing circular mutual signing with scoped cross-attestation
  + Honesty and Security should independently recognize one another's exact faculty identity and bind their own judgments to the same startup evidence and trust-catalogue root
  + neither faculty should sign on behalf of the other, mint the other's authority, share a signing identity, or make the other's result valid
  + Observer closes startup only when both scoped attestations identify the expected counterpart and bind the same contract, measurement, runtime generation, and catalogue root
  + the catalogue should use cryptographic digests and digital signatures rather than CRC as its trust mechanism
  = concise: cross-recognize counterpart identity, independently sign separate claims, then let Observer seal their agreement over one evidence root

& [CRCBoundary] distinguishes corruption checking from trust
  + a cyclic redundancy check is efficient for detecting common accidental transmission or storage errors
  + CRC is not collision resistant and an adversary who changes a file can compute a matching replacement CRC
  + CRC therefore cannot establish SOP identity, authenticity, authority, provenance, or resistance to intentional substitution
  + an optional CRC32C may accompany transport framing for early accidental-error detection only
  + SHA-256 or a separately selected cryptographic digest binds file and manifest content under the current Cantor profile
  + a digital signature binds the cryptographic root to an identified authorized signer and scope
  - never: promote successful CRC comparison into a trust, authenticity, or admission result

& [TrustAuthorityDelineation] preserves existing signer roles
  + [authority_signer] signs the semantic authority, validity interval, scope, source root, semantic root, and package root
  + [compiler_signer] independently signs compiler identity, version, dependency lock, package root, and proof identities
  + [Honesty_attester] signs only a runtime correspondence judgment over measured faculty, catalogue, contract, and generation identity
  + [Security_attester] signs only a runtime integrity and admissibility judgment over the same roots plus authority, isolation, lifecycle, and effect state
  + [Observer_sealer] verifies all required signatures and emits final KernelIdentityAttestation or failure
  - never: let a faculty runtime attestation authorize SOP source content or replace authority and compiler signatures
  - never: let authority and compiler signing keys collapse into one identity

& [FacultyIdentityRecord] makes counterpart recognition exact
  + [faculty] is Honesty or Security
  + [program_id] and [program_digest] bind exact compiled faculty behavior
  + [interpreter_or_binary_digest] binds the execution substrate when material
  + [profile_and_version] bind data and decision semantics
  + [attestation_signer_id] and [verification_key_ref] bind the scoped runtime attestation identity
  + [role_scope] limits the propositions this faculty may attest
  + [authority_ceiling] forbids semantic or effect authority expansion
  + [validity] and [revocation_ref] bound lifecycle
  + [expected_counterpart_ref] identifies the exact counterpart record expected in this kernel generation
  + [separation_profile] states same-process logical, separate-process software, separate-host, hardware-backed, or externally verified isolation

& [SOPTrustCatalogue] is a signed canonical inventory rather than a flat path and CRC table
  + [catalogue_id] and [catalogue_version] are immutable
  + [canonical_encoding_profile] fixes ordering, normalization, field representation, and digest domains
  + [entry_set] is sorted by stable file or package identity rather than operating-system enumeration order
  + [catalogue_root_digest] commits the complete canonical catalogue
  + [authority_signature_set] binds the root to authorized semantic and compilation identities
  + [validity_interval], [revocation_root], [dependency_lock_root], and [policy_root] bind lifecycle and trust policy
  + [allowed_startup_pattern_roots] identify the OHS kernel and every post-confirmation pattern package eligible to load
  + [unavailable_or_excluded_set] prevents silent omission from looking like trusted absence
  + [previous_catalogue_ref] creates append-only version and revocation lineage
  - never: make human-readable file path the stable authority identity

& [SOPTrustCatalogueEntry] binds one file or package member exactly
  + [entry_id] is stable and content-addressed where appropriate
  + [package_id], [file_id], and [semantic_role] bind containment and intended use
  + [display_path] is inspection metadata rather than sole identity
  + [byte_length] and [cryptographic_digest] bind exact file bytes
  + [source_root], [semantic_root], and [package_root] connect the entry to compiled meaning and package authority
  + [compiler_profile], [schema_profile], and [pattern_role] identify interpretation
  + [authority_scope] and [allowed_use] prevent a trusted file from becoming universally authorized
  + [dependency_refs] bind required catalogue or package entries
  + [certificate_ref], [validity], [lifecycle], and [revocation_ref] bind admission state
  + [optional_transport_crc] is explicitly non-authoritative when present

& [CatalogueRootShape] may be canonical manifest or Merkle index
  + a signed digest of one canonical ordered manifest is sufficient for the first bounded implementation
  + a Merkle tree may later provide selective membership and non-membership proofs for large catalogues
  + either shape requires domain-separated leaves, deterministic ordering, duplicate rejection, exact count, and root signature
  + Merkle structure does not itself establish signer authority, content truth, lifecycle, or use scope
  = candidate: start with the existing canonical package and source-root model before adding Merkle complexity from measured need

& [CrossRecognitionRecord] proves counterpart identity without delegated trust
  + [round_id] binds one startup confirmation round
  + [observer_frame_ref] identifies the candidate self frame
  + [catalogue_root_digest] and [runtime_generation_digest] must be identical in both faculty records
  + [self_faculty_identity_ref] binds the attester
  + [observed_counterpart_identity_ref] binds the exact counterpart measured
  + [counterpart_match] is exact, mismatch, revoked, stale, unavailable, or unresolved
  + [separation_evidence] states what independence was actually achieved
  + [measurement_ref] prevents self-asserted counterpart identity
  + [signature] authenticates only the attester's scoped observation and judgment
  - never: interpret counterpart_match as adoption of the counterpart's conclusions

& [HonestyAttestation] is a domain-separated correspondence claim
  + binds startup contract, measurement, catalogue root, runtime generation, Honesty identity, Security observed identity, evidence, uncertainty, and result
  + confirms that named package and faculty identities correspond to measured cryptographic and semantic roots
  + identifies omissions, substitutions, mismatched roots, unsupported catalogue membership, and unavailable evidence
  + cannot claim isolation, effect safety, or Security approval

& [SecurityAttestation] is a domain-separated integrity and admissibility claim
  + binds the same startup contract, measurement, catalogue root, and runtime generation
  + binds Security identity, Honesty observed identity, trust policy, revocation state, authority ceiling, isolation profile, open channels, and effect boundary
  + identifies shared-key collapse, signer-role collision, stale catalogue, unauthorized dependency, unmeasured input, and premature activation
  + cannot claim semantic truth or Honesty approval

& [ObserverJointClosure] is intersection rather than signature substitution
  + require expected Honesty and Security signer identities under distinct signature domains
  + require both attestations to bind byte-identical round, contract, measurement, catalogue root, and generation identities
  + require each counterpart recognition to match the pinned FacultyIdentityRecord
  + require both scoped results to satisfy the startup policy independently
  + preserve disagreement and fail closed rather than asking one faculty to countersign the other
  + seal the pair into KernelIdentityAttestation without expanding either scope

& [IndependenceBoundary] makes strength claims truthful
  + two keys inside one process provide domain and role separation but not strong compromise independence
  + separate processes with distinct keys improve fault isolation while sharing a host threat boundary
  + separate hosts improve software independence while introducing authenticated transport and synchronization requirements
  + hardware-backed or externally verified keys may strengthen key possession and measurement claims under their exact platform guarantees
  + the separation profile must accompany every attestation so Observer does not infer a stronger trust model than was used

& [CatalogueLifecycle] is immutable and boot-bound
  + catalogue update produces a new version, root digest, signatures, validity, and predecessor reference
  + added, removed, changed, revoked, stale, and superseded entries remain explicitly accounted
  + any active catalogue-root change invalidates the current KernelIdentityAttestation and requires a new OHS round
  + no faculty may edit an admitted catalogue in place
  + recovery catalogue roots remain distinct from normal runtime roots

& [CurrentImplementationFit] is strong at package trust and absent at faculty cross-attestation
  + current CantorRecognitionCertificate already contains package, semantic, and source root digests plus separate authority and compiler signatures
  + current TrustStore already separates signer roles, compiler allowlists, dependency lock, revocation, and stale package state
  + current compiler and admission logic already reject signer collapse, digest mismatch, lifecycle failure, and dependency drift
  + current package inventory can seed a bounded SOPTrustCatalogue without CRC
  + current faculties do not have attestation signer identities, cross-recognition records, or a joint startup closure
  + current single-process faculty execution cannot claim independent compromise resistance

& [CandidateRequirementSet] is OHTC-001 through OHTC-017
  + [OHTC-001] define scoped cross-recognition without mutual authorization or signature substitution
  + [OHTC-002] retain separate authority, compiler, Honesty, Security, and Observer signature domains and roles
  + [OHTC-003] define FacultyIdentityRecord with exact program, substrate, version, key, role, lifecycle, counterpart, and separation profile
  + [OHTC-004] define immutable signed canonical SOPTrustCatalogue and ordered catalogue entries
  + [OHTC-005] bind each entry by cryptographic digest, byte length, package roots, semantics, scope, dependency, certificate, lifecycle, and revocation
  + [OHTC-006] forbid CRC from serving as identity, authenticity, or trust evidence
  + [OHTC-007] permit optional CRC only as explicitly non-authoritative transport-error metadata
  + [OHTC-008] use the current canonical signed-manifest root first and add Merkle indexing only from measured selective-proof requirements
  + [OHTC-009] define CrossRecognitionRecord bound to one round, measurement, catalogue root, generation, counterpart, and separation evidence
  + [OHTC-010] define domain-separated HonestyAttestation and SecurityAttestation with non-overlapping claims
  + [OHTC-011] require Observer closure to match all common roots byte-exactly and preserve disagreement
  + [OHTC-012] prohibit shared signing identity, faculty key substitution, self-issued counterpart identity, and runtime attestation as package authority
  + [OHTC-013] version catalogue updates immutably and invalidate boot attestation on any root change
  + [OHTC-014] bind excluded, unavailable, revoked, stale, removed, and superseded entries explicitly
  + [OHTC-015] carry separation-profile strength into every trust result and prohibit independence inflation
  + [OHTC-016] test modified-file same-CRC substitution, cryptographic mismatch, split catalogue roots, signer collision, revoked counterpart, stale catalogue, omitted entry, dependency drift, key rotation, replay, and valid co-attestation
  + [OHTC-017] prove compatibility or migration with current recognition certificates, TrustStore, package manifests, self-hosted corpus, and PreparedRuntime generation identity

& [Decision] is add cross-attestation and trust-catalogue formation to OHSK preparation
  + [canonical_authority] is absent
  + [implementation_authority] is absent
  + [key_authority] is absent
  + [signed_catalogue] is absent
  + [schedule_effect] OHSK-SJS-01 must process OHTC-001 through OHTC-017 before choosing keys, signatures, catalogue schema, or Merkle structure
  + [next] audit current certificate statements, trust-store roots, manifest inventories, key custody, signature domains, revocation, update, and process-isolation profiles
